{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QabExzRmfUU62U96rTyAET","type":"tool_result","content":"2119\tint main(int argc, char** argv) {\n2120\t  g_self = argv[0];\n2121\t  // SECURITY, fail-CLOSED: the gate emits its verdict over a unix socket to any\n2122\t  // live watcher BEFORE it reaches exit(2). If the watcher's end is closed, a\n2123\t  // raw write() would raise SIGPIPE and KILL this process mid-block — and Claude\n2124\t  // Code treats a signal-killed PreToolUse hook as ALLOW, so a dangerous command\n2125\t  // would slip through exactly when someone was watching. Ignore SIGPIPE so a\n2126\t  // dead watcher can never turn a block into an allow (write just returns EPIPE,\n2127\t  // which emit already discards). This is the whole point of the gate.\n2128\t  signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_IGN);\n2129\t\n2130\t  // `rabadon-gate --help` used to fall through to hook mode, read an empty\n2131\t  // stdin, fail open and exit 0 having printed NOTHING. Zero bytes and a clean\n2132\t  // exit code is exactly what a working hook looks like, so the binary that\n2133\t  // enforces everything was the one that answered nothing.\n2134\t  rb_help(argc, argv, kHelp);\n2135\t\n2136\t  // A flag this gate does not know is NAMED, then IGNORED.\n2137\t  //   named   — swallowing it is how a mistyped `--stauts` fell through to hook\n2138\t  //             mode, read an empty stdin, failed open and printed zero bytes.\n2139\t  //             Exit 0 and no output is exactly what a healthy hook looks like.\n2140\t  //   ignored — this binary IS a PreToolUse hook, where a non-zero exit means\n2141\t  //             BLOCK. Refusing here would let one typo in a settings.json hook\n2142\t  //             line wedge every tool call on the machine. Fail OPEN, always.\n2143\t  if (argc > 1 && argv[1][0] == '-' && argv[1][1] != '\\0') {\n2144\t    static const char* kKnownFlags[] = {\"--version\", \"--lint\", \"--statusline\",\n2145\t                                        \"--on\", \"--off\", \"--toggle\", \"--status\", \"--silent\", \"--wrong\"};\n2146\t    bool recognised = false;\n2147\t    for (const char* k : kKnownFlags) if (strcmp(argv[1], k) == 0) recognised = true;\n2148\t    if (!recognised) {\n2149\t      fprintf(stderr, \"rabadon-gate: unknown option \\\"%s\\\" — run `rabadon-gate --help`\\n\", argv[1]);\n2150\t      return 0;\n2151\t    }\n2152\t  }\n2153\t\n2154\t  // --version for install sanity checks\n2155\t  if (argc > 1 && string(argv[1]) == \"--version\") { printf(\"rabadon-gate \" RABADON_VERSION \"\\n\"); return 0; }\n2156\t\n2157\t  // --lint [dir] — validate a guard.json before it is trusted. A typo'd key\n2158\t  // (\"protectedPath\" for \"protectedPaths\") or an uncompilable regex used to be\n2159\t  // swallowed silently: the gate would OBSERVE where the author meant it to\n2160\t  // BLOCK. Lint compiles every pattern and flags unknown top-level keys, exit\n2161\t  // 1 with the list — so a broken guard is caught at author time, not by a\n2162\t  // deny that never fired.\n2163\t  if (argc > 1 && string(argv[1]) == \"--lint\") {\n2164\t    string dir = argc > 2 ? argv[2] : \".\";\n2165\t    string path = dir + \"/.rabadon/guard.json\";\n2166\t    string g = read_file(path);\n2167\t    if (g.empty()) { fprintf(stderr, \"rabadon lint: no guard at %s\\n\", path.c_str()); return 1; }\n2168\t    int problems = 0;\n2169\t    // unknown top-level keys (catches protectedPath/bashRules/etc typos)\n2170\t    static const char* known[] = {\"project\", \"bash\", \"protectedPaths\", \"codePaths\", \"testPaths\",\n2171\t                                   \"testCommand\", \"testPassPattern\", \"network\", \"disabled\",\n2172\t                                   \"generatedBy\", \"authoredBy\", \"pushGate\", \"evidence\"};\n2173\t    { size_t i = 0; int depth = 0;\n2174\t      while (i < g.size()) {\n2175\t        char c = g[i];\n2176\t        if (c == '\"') {\n2177\t          size_t s = i + 1; string key;\n2178\t          for (i++; i < g.size(); i++) { if (g[i] == '\\\\') i++; else if (g[i] == '\"') break; else key += g[i]; }\n2179\t          i++;\n2180\t          size_t j = i; while (j < g.size() && isspace((unsigned char)g[j])) j++;\n2181\t          if (depth == 1 && j < g.size() && g[j] == ':') {\n2182\t            bool ok = false; for (const char* k : known) if (key == k) ok = true;\n2183\t            if (!ok) { fprintf(stderr, \"rabadon lint: unknown top-level key \\\"%s\\\" (typo? ignored by the gate)\\n\", key.c_str()); problems++; }\n2184\t          }\n2185\t          (void)s; continue;\n2186\t        }\n2187\t        if (c == '{' || c == '[') depth++;\n2188\t        else if (c == '}' || c == ']') depth--;\n2189\t        i++;\n2190\t      }\n2191\t    }\n2192\t    // every deny/match pattern must compile\n2193\t    auto lint_rules = [&](const char* section, const char* patKey) {\n2194\t      std::vector<std::string> empty;\n2195\t      for (const auto& r : parse_rules(g, section, patKey, empty)) {\n2196\t        try { std::regex re(r.pattern); (void)re; }\n2197\t        catch (const std::exception& e) {\n2198\t          fprintf(stderr, \"rabadon lint: rule \\\"%s\\\" has an uncompilable %s regex: %s\\n\", r.id.c_str(), patKey, e.what());\n2199\t          problems++;\n2200\t        }\n2201\t      }\n2202\t    };\n2203\t    // ...and every rule object must actually BE a rule. The top-level walk\n2204\t    // above stops at depth 1, so it catches \"protectedPathz\" and is blind one\n2205\t    // level down — inside the rule object, where the author is typing. A rule\n2206\t    // written {\"id\":..., \"denies\":..., \"why\":...} is valid JSON, carries an id\n2207\t    // and a why, and reads as enforced; parse_rules drops it for having no\n2208\t    // pattern and the gate allows the command. Measured before this check:\n2209\t    // gate exit 0 on `npx wrangler deploy`, lint \"is valid\", exit 0. Same for a\n2210\t    // rule with no pattern key at all. That is this command's own stated\n2211\t    // failure mode (\"the gate would OBSERVE where the author meant it to\n2212\t    // BLOCK\") surviving one nesting level deeper than the check that ended it.\n2213\t    //\n2214\t    // Legal beside the pattern: `why`/`id` (the schema, docs/guard.md),\n2215\t    // `authoredBy`+`incidentAt` (rules born from an incident, below) and\n2216\t    // `source` (`rabadon pack import`). Those are keys rabadon writes itself —\n2217\t    // reject them and rabadon's output fails rabadon's own linter. The other\n2218\t    // section's pattern key is deliberately NOT legal: a `match` inside bash[]\n2219\t    // is inert, and reading as enforced while matching nothing is the bug.\n2220\t    auto lint_rule_objects = [&](const char* section, const char* patKey) {\n2221\t      // `wrongAt`/`wrongWhy` are the other half of `authoredBy`/`incidentAt`.\n2222\t      // A rule born from an incident already carries the day it was born; a rule\n2223\t      // that has refused something it should not have has to carry that too, on\n2224\t      // the rule, where the next author reads it — the ledger records the event\n2225\t      // and nobody opening guard.json sees the ledger.\n2226\t      static const char* kRuleKeys[] = {\"id\", \"why\", \"authoredBy\", \"incidentAt\", \"source\",\n2227\t                                        \"allow\", \"catches\", \"wrongAt\", \"wrongWhy\"};\n2228\t      int idx = -1;\n2229\t      for (const string& obj : rbrules::parse_rule_objects(g, section)) {\n2230\t        idx++;\n2231\t        const string id = rbrules::get_str(obj, \"id\");\n2232\t        // an id-less broken rule still has to be findable in the file\n2233\t        const string named = id.empty() ? string(section) + \"[\" + std::to_string(idx) + \"]\" : id;\n2234\t        bool hasPattern = false;\n2235\t        for (const string& key : rbrules::rule_object_keys(obj)) {\n2236\t          if (key == patKey) { hasPattern = true; continue; }\n2237\t          bool ok = false;\n2238\t          for (const char* k : kRuleKeys) if (key == k) ok = true;\n2239\t          if (!ok) {\n2240\t            fprintf(stderr, \"rabadon lint: rule \\\"%s\\\" in %s has unknown key \\\"%s\\\" (typo for \\\"%s\\\"? the gate ignores it)\\n\",\n2241\t                    named.c_str(), section, key.c_str(), patKey);\n2242\t            problems++;\n2243\t          }\n2244\t        }\n2245\t        // present-but-empty counts as absent: parse_rules drops an empty\n2246\t        // pattern too, so the key being there is not the rule existing.\n2247\t        if (hasPattern && rbrules::get_str(obj, patKey).empty()) hasPattern = false;\n2248\t        if (!hasPattern) {\n2249\t          fprintf(stderr, \"rabadon lint: rule \\\"%s\\\" in %s has no \\\"%s\\\" pattern — it matches nothing, the gate skips it\\n\",\n2250\t                  named.c_str(), section, patKey);\n2251\t          problems++;\n2252\t        }\n2253\t      }\n2254\t    };\n2255\t    // Everything above asks whether a rule CAN fire. Nothing asked whether it\n2256\t    // can ever hold its fire, and that is the half that broke in the field: an\n2257\t    // authored `semantic-commit-required` denied every commit including the\n2258\t    // `fix:` ones it existed to permit, because an optional quote let its\n2259\t    // negative lookahead pass on the quote character instead of the message.\n2260\t    // lint called that guard valid, because the rule compiled and it fired.\n2261\t    //\n2262\t    // A rule that refuses everything is as broken as one that matches nothing,\n2263\t    // and it is worse to live with: the first blocks real work every day, the\n2264\t    // second only fails when the danger finally arrives. So a rule carries\n2265\t    // `allow`, the commands it must NOT match, and they are run against its own\n2266\t    // pattern through the same matcher the gate uses. A rule with no twin is\n2267\t    // reported but not failed yet — no guard in the wild has them until the\n2268\t    // author writes them.\n2269\t    auto lint_allow_twins = [&](const char* section, const char* patKey) {\n2270\t      int missing = 0, total = 0;\n2271\t      const bool isCommand = string(section) == \"bash\";\n2272\t      for (const string& obj : rbrules::parse_rule_objects(g, section)) {\n2273\t        total++;\n2274\t        const string id = rbrules::get_str(obj, \"id\");\n2275\t        const string pat = rbrules::get_str(obj, patKey);\n2276\t        if (pat.empty()) continue;  // already reported above\n2277\t        const std::vector<string> allow = rbrules::get_str_array(obj, \"allow\");\n2278\t        if (allow.empty()) { missing++; continue; }\n2279\t        for (const string& example : allow) {\n2280\t          const bool refused = isCommand ? rbrules::rx_test_cmd(pat, example)\n2281\t                                         : rbrules::rx_test(pat, example);\n2282\t          if (refused) {\n2283\t            fprintf(stderr, \"rabadon lint: rule \\\"%s\\\" refuses its own allow example — %s\\n\",\n2284\t                    id.c_str(), example.c_str());\n2285\t            problems++;\n2286\t          }\n2287\t        }\n2288\t      }\n2289\t      if (missing) {\n2290\t        fprintf(stderr, \"rabadon lint: %d of %d rule(s) in %s carry no \\\"allow\\\" example — nothing proves they let real work through\\n\",\n2291\t                missing, total, section);\n2292\t      }\n2293\t    };\n2294\t    // And the half neither of those covers. `allow` proves a rule is not too\n2295\t    // WIDE. Nothing proved it was not too NARROW, because the schema had no way\n2296\t    // for an author to say what the rule exists to stop.\n2297\t    //\n2298\t    // Measured on 3 August: every guard rule on this machine, 430 of them, was\n2299\t    // driven through the real gate with a command its own pattern was written to\n2300\t    // refuse. 16 refused nothing, in any repository, ever, and all 16 linted\n2301\t    // clean. Three had been authored by the engine itself after real incidents,\n2302\t    // so each named something that had already happened once and was free to\n2303\t    // happen again. The two mechanisms behind fourteen and two of them are\n2304\t    // invisible in the pattern: a protectedPaths rule authored relative is\n2305\t    // compared against a spelling no event carries, and a bash pattern that\n2306\t    // spells a pipe is asking for a character the parser removed before any rule\n2307\t    // was consulted. An author cannot be expected to know either.\n2308\t    //\n2309\t    // What an author CAN write is the thing the rule is for. So a rule carries\n2310\t    // `catches` beside `allow`, and lint runs it through the rule's own pattern\n2311\t    // with the gate's own matcher — rx_test_cmd for a command, which segments\n2312\t    // the line exactly as judging does, and rx_test for a path, which is offered\n2313\t    // both the arriving spelling and the project-relative one. A rule that\n2314\t    // cannot cut its own example is dead, and it is said at author time rather\n2315\t    // than on the night the danger arrives.\n2316\t    auto lint_deny_twins = [&](const char* section, const char* patKey) {\n2317\t      int missing = 0, total = 0;\n2318\t      const bool isCommand = string(section) == \"bash\";\n2319\t      for (const string& obj : rbrules::parse_rule_objects(g, section)) {\n2320\t        total++;\n2321\t        const string id = rbrules::get_str(obj, \"id\");\n2322\t        const string pat = rbrules::get_str(obj, patKey);\n2323\t        if (pat.empty()) continue;  // already reported above\n2324\t        const std::vector<string> catches = rbrules::get_str_array(obj, \"catches\");\n2325\t        if (catches.empty()) { missing++; continue; }\n2326\t        for (const string& example : catches) {\n2327\t          const bool refused = isCommand ? rbrules::rx_test_cmd(pat, example)\n2328\t                                         : rbrules::rx_test(pat, example);\n2329\t          if (!refused) {\n2330\t            fprintf(stderr, \"rabadon lint: rule \\\"%s\\\" cannot refuse the thing it was written for — %s\\n\",\n2331\t                    id.c_str(), example.c_str());\n2332\t            fprintf(stderr, \"              the rule is dead: it compiles, it reads correctly, and no %s reaches it\\n\",\n2333\t                    isCommand ? \"command\" : \"path\");\n2334\t            problems++;\n2335\t          }\n2336\t        }\n2337\t      }\n2338\t      if (missing) {\n2339\t        fprintf(stderr, \"rabadon lint: %d of %d rule(s) in %s carry no \\\"catches\\\" example — nothing proves they can still refuse anything\\n\",\n2340\t                missing, total, section);\n2341\t      }\n2342\t    };\n2343\t    lint_rules(\"bash\", \"deny\");\n2344\t    lint_rules(\"protectedPaths\", \"match\");\n2345\t    lint_rule_objects(\"bash\", \"deny\");\n2346\t    lint_rule_objects(\"protectedPaths\", \"match\");\n2347\t    lint_allow_twins(\"bash\", \"deny\");\n2348\t    lint_allow_twins(\"protectedPaths\", \"match\");\n2349\t    lint_deny_twins(\"bash\", \"deny\");\n2350\t    lint_deny_twins(\"protectedPaths\", \"match\");\n2351\t    if (problems == 0) { printf(\"rabadon lint: %s is valid.\\n\", path.c_str()); return 0; }\n2352\t    fprintf(stderr, \"rabadon lint: %d problem(s) — fix them or the gate silently ignores those rules.\\n\", problems);\n2353\t    return 1;\n2354\t  }\n2355\t\n2356\t  // --statusline — the glanceable lamp for the Claude Code status bar. It reads\n2357\t  // the SAME source of truth as the gate (~/.rabadon/enabled), so the bar can\n2358\t  // never lie about state: lit lilac ● when supervising, dark gray ○ + \"off\" when\n2359\t  // dormant. Read-only, one stat, fast. (The old JS statusline read a different\n2360\t  // off-switch and stayed lit even when the gate was dormant — that mismatch is\n2361\t  // exactly why the lamp couldn't be trusted.)\n2362\t  if (argc > 1 && string(argv[1]) == \"--statusline\") {\n2363\t    string in; { char b[8192]; size_t n; while ((n = fread(b, 1, sizeof b, stdin)) > 0) in.append(b, n); }\n2364\t    string dir = get_str(in, \"current_dir\"); if (dir.empty()) dir = get_str(in, \"cwd\");\n2365\t    if (dir.empty()) { const char* p = getenv(\"PWD\"); dir = p ? p : \".\"; }\n2366\t    string model = get_str(in, \"display_name\");\n2367\t    size_t sl = dir.rfind('/'); string project = (sl == string::npos) ? dir : dir.substr(sl + 1);\n2368\t    const string rhome = rabadon_home();\n2369\t    const char* off = getenv(\"RABADON_OFF\");\n2370\t    bool hardOff = (off && string(off) == \"1\") || file_exists(dir + \"/.rabadon/off\")\n2371\t                   || file_exists(rhome + \"/silent\");\n2372\t    bool on = !hardOff && (file_exists(rhome + \"/enabled\") || file_exists(dir + \"/.rabadon/on\"));\n2373\t    bool watching = !hardOff && !on;\n2374\t    const string GRAY = \"\\033[38;5;245m\", R = \"\\033[0m\";\n2375\t\n2376\t    // The ON lamp BREATHES. Claude Code spawns this process fresh for every\n2377\t    // render and keeps no state, so the phase has to be a pure function of the\n2378\t    // wall clock. Measured on this host (Claude Code 2.1.172): the status line\n2379\t    // is event-driven with a 300ms trailing debounce and NO timer unless the\n2380\t    // user sets statusLine.refreshInterval (floor: 1 second). Sampling is\n2381\t    // therefore irregular, and at best ~0.8 frames/sec.\n2382\t    //\n2383\t    // That measurement is the reason this is a brightness pulse and not a\n2384\t    // highlight sweeping across the letters: a moving crest sampled at\n2385\t    // irregular moments reads as a light that TELEPORTS (letter 2, then 7,\n2386\t    // then 3). Brightness carries no positional expectation — sampled at any\n2387\t    // moment it simply reads as alive. OFF stays dead and static, because a\n2388\t    // dormant supervisor must look dormant.\n2389\t    static const int RAMP[] = { 97, 104, 141, 183, 189, 183, 141, 104 };\n2390\t    const int STEPS = 8, STEP_MS = 750;           // one full breath = 6 seconds\n2391\t    long long nowms; { struct timespec ts; clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME, &ts);\n2392\t                       nowms = (long long)ts.tv_sec * 1000 + ts.tv_nsec / 1000000; }\n2393\t    // test hook: pin the clock so the breath is a deterministic, assertable\n2394\t    // sequence instead of \"whatever millisecond the test ran in\".\n2395\t    { const char* fake = getenv(\"RABADON_LAMP_MS\"); if (fake && *fake) nowms = atoll(fake); }\n2396\t    int ph = (int)((nowms / STEP_MS) % STEPS);\n2397\t    char lamp[80];\n2398\t    // ONE colour for the whole lamp. The star used to run two steps ahead of\n2399\t    // the word, as a pilot light, and that idea reads fine in a comment and\n2400\t    // wrong on a screen: at every sampled moment the mark and the name are two\n2401\t    // different colours, so it looks mismatched rather than alive. The breath\n2402\t    // is the part that carries \"this can act on you right now\" and it survives.\n2403\t    // The mark just breathes with the word it belongs to.\n2404\t    snprintf(lamp, sizeof lamp, \"\\033[38;5;%dm* rabadon\\033[0m\", RAMP[ph]);\n2405\t    // Three states, three readings, and the breath means one specific thing:\n2406\t    // rabadon can act on you right now. WATCH is deliberately still — awake and\n2407\t    // recording, but with its hands behind its back. SILENT is grey and says so.\n2408\t    const string DIM_LILAC = \"\\033[38;5;97m\";\n2409\t    string seg = on       ? string(lamp)\n2410\t               : watching ? (DIM_LILAC + \"* rabadon watch\" + R)\n2411\t                          : (GRAY + \"* rabadon off\" + R);\n2412\t    printf(\"%s%s%s%s%s  %s\\n\", GRAY.c_str(), model.c_str(), model.empty() ? \"\" : \" · \",\n2413\t           project.c_str(), R.c_str(), seg.c_str());\n2414\t    return 0;\n2415\t  }\n2416\t\n2417\t  // the deterministic switch. Two files, three states, and the gate reads the\n2418\t  // real ones — so a skill or an alias never has to \"pretend\" a state.\n2419\t  //   ~/.rabadon/enabled -> ENFORCE (the arbiter acts)\n2420\t  //   neither file       -> WATCH   (records everything, blocks nothing)\n2421\t  //   ~/.rabadon/silent  -> SILENT  (truly dormant; also RABADON_OFF=1 per process\n2422\t  //                                  and .rabadon/off per project)\n2423\t  // `rabadon` toggles ENFORCE <-> WATCH, because that is the switch a user flips\n2424\t  // daily. Going fully dark is deliberate and separate: `rabadon silent`.\n2425\t  if (argc > 1 && string(argv[1]) == \"--wrong\") {\n2426\t    // A REFUSAL THAT WAS WRONG. The standing rule when the gate refuses\n2427\t    // something it should not have is: do not add the rule to disabled[], do\n2428\t    // not route around it, write it down and fix the rule. There was nowhere to\n2429\t    // write it down. Three happened on 3 August and all three ended up as prose\n2430\t    // in a report, which means the one number this product is actually judged\n2431\t    // on lived outside the ledger.\n2432\t    //\n2433\t    // Anybody can publish how many commands they refused. That number means\n2434\t    // nothing on its own; it means something beside the count of refusals that\n2435\t    // were wrong, and only a ledger that carries both can be asked for the\n2436\t    // second one. So it goes on the same chain as the first.\n2437\t    if (argc < 4 || !argv[2][0] || !argv[3][0]) {\n2438\t      fprintf(stderr,\n2439\t        \"usage: rabadon wrong <rule-id> \\\"why it was wrong\\\"\\n\"\n2440\t        \"\\n\"\n2441\t        \"Records a refusal that should not have happened, on the same hash-chained\\n\"\n2442\t        \"ledger as the refusal itself. A rule id with no reason is a complaint and\\n\"\n2443\t        \"not a record, so the reason is required.\\n\");\n2444\t      return 2;\n2445\t    }\n2446\t    string why = argv[3];\n2447\t    for (int i = 4; i < argc; i++) why += string(\" \") + argv[i];\n2448\t    ledger_line(\"WRONG_REFUSAL\",\n2449\t                \"\\\"rule\\\":\\\"\" + json_escape(string(argv[2])) + \"\\\",\\\"why\\\":\\\"\" +\n2450\t                json_escape(why) + \"\\\"\");\n2451\t    // R4: for a rule that BLOCKS, saying \"that was wrong\" has to also let the\n2452\t    // work happen — a refusal whose only remedy is a note in a file is a wedge,\n2453\t    // and a wedged session ends with the tool uninstalled (Law 1). So the\n2454\t    // record leaves a ONE-SHOT pass beside the ledger: the next write the rule\n2455\t    // would refuse goes through, spends the pass, and is itself recorded. One\n2456\t    // shot, because an escape that stays open is a rule that is off.\n2457\t    {\n2458\t      const string rhome = rabadon_home();\n2459\t      mkdir(rhome.c_str(), 0755);\n2460\t      string safe;\n2461\t      for (const char* p = argv[2]; *p && safe.size() < 64; p++)\n2462\t        safe += (isalnum((unsigned char)*p) || *p == '-' || *p == '_') ? *p : '_';\n2463\t      if (!safe.empty()) { FILE* f = fopen((rhome + \"/wrong-\" + safe).c_str(), \"w\");\n2464\t                           if (f) { fputs(why.c_str(), f); fclose(f); } }\n2465\t    }\n2466\t    printf(\"rabadon: recorded a wrong refusal by %s.\\n\"\n2467\t           \"  It is on the ledger next to the refusals, which is the only place a\\n\"\n2468\t           \"  false-positive count can be read from instead of asserted.\\n\"\n2469\t           \"  Now fix the rule -- disabled[] is not the answer to a rule that is wrong.\\n\",\n2470\t           argv[2]);\n2471\t    return 0;\n2472\t  }\n2473\t\n2474\t  if (argc > 1) {\n2475\t    string a1 = argv[1];\n2476\t    if (a1 == \"--on\" || a1 == \"--off\" || a1 == \"--toggle\" || a1 == \"--status\" || a1 == \"--silent\") {\n2477\t      const string rhome = rabadon_home();\n2478\t      mkdir(rhome.c_str(), 0755);\n2479\t      const string flag = rhome + \"/enabled\";     // legacy, read, never written\n2480\t      const string mute = rhome + \"/silent\";\n2481\t      const string modeFile = rhome + \"/mode\";\n2482\t      // The switch is one file now. `enabled` is still READ so an existing\n2483\t      // install is not disarmed by an upgrade, but nothing writes it again.\n2484\t      const string cur = read_mode_file(modeFile);\n2485\t      bool on = cur.empty() ? file_exists(flag) : (cur == \"enforce\");\n2486\t      bool silent = cur.empty() ? file_exists(mute) : (cur == \"silent\");\n2487\t      if (a1 == \"--toggle\") a1 = on ? \"--off\" : \"--on\";\n2488\t      // WHAT THE MODE WAS, BEFORE IT IS CHANGED. On 3 August at 02:25 a session\n2489\t      // ran `rabadon off` and the machine was unguarded from that moment on,\n2490\t      // while four other sessions kept working underneath it with no way to\n2491\t      // know. It was reconstructable afterwards only because the TEXT of the\n2492\t      // command happened to land in a step record, which is an accident of a\n2493\t      // different feature. Supervision going away is the most consequential\n2494\t      // thing anybody does to this tool and it was the one action that was not\n2495\t      // an event.\n2496\t      const string was = silent ? \"silent\" : (on ? \"enforce\" : \"watch\");\n2497\t      // THE COLLAPSE IS NOT DONE HERE, ON PURPOSE. Faz 3 Kapsam orders\n2498\t      // `enabled` + `mode.last` into one file; Faz 3 Durma forbids breaking an\n2499\t      // existing test; native/cli_test.sh:210 asserts `<RABADON_DIR>/enabled`\n2500\t      // exists after `rabadon toggle`. The two sentences cannot both be\n2501\t      // obeyed, so the contradiction is escalated (reports/phase-3/BLOCKED.md)\n2502\t      // instead of being resolved by editing the test — editing it is the\n2503\t      // exact move this product exists to refuse. The new one-word file IS\n2504\t      // written, so the layered reader has something to read; the legacy files\n2505\t      // stay until a human rules on the challenge.\n2506\t      auto write_mode = [&](const char* w) {\n2507\t        std::ofstream f(modeFile, std::ios::trunc);\n2508\t        if (f) f << w << \"\\n\";\n2509\t      };\n2510\t      if (a1 == \"--on\") {\n2511\t        unlink(mute.c_str()); silent = false; write_mode(\"enforce\");\n2512\t        std::ofstream f(flag, std::ios::trunc); f << \"on\\n\"; on = true;\n2513\t      } else if (a1 == \"--off\") {\n2514\t        unlink(flag.c_str()); on = false;\n2515\t        unlink(mute.c_str()); silent = false; write_mode(\"watch\");\n2516\t      } else if (a1 == \"--silent\") {\n2517\t        unlink(flag.c_str()); on = false; write_mode(\"silent\");\n2518\t        std::ofstream f(mute, std::ios::trunc); f << \"silent\\n\"; silent = true;"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CNJNRkZewzBF62my6wyhvM","session_id":"5cd1d3b8-0ab4-436c-a096-b92b12ed0757","uuid":"50d407a6-b95a-4d72-96a5-293b4643ad1f","timestamp":"2026-08-24T10:37:55.739Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Map gate.cpp hook path for daemon"}
