{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0149AUvzptdhVwn4BwesqeCQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1153\tstruct State {\n1154\t  string path;      // <project>/.rabadon/state.json — the shared facts\n1155\t  string sessDir;   // <project>/.rabadon/sessions   — one file per session\n1156\t  string sessKey;   // the file this process owns in there\n1157\t  string legacyKey; // the sid16 this session had inside the old shared map\n1158\t  long long lastCodeEdit = 0, lastDiagAt = 0;\n1159\t  // rabadon RAN the suite and read the real exit code itself — the push gate's\n1160\t  // own fork, or the always-on net's. This is the only test result one session\n1161\t  // may act on because another session produced it, and it is shared for\n1162\t  // exactly that reason. Before the verified/watched split, `go test -run\n1163\t  // TestNothingMatchesThis ./...` printed `ok vac 0.142s [no tests to run]`,\n1164\t  // exited 0, refreshed the stamp, and the next push went out over a red suite.\n1165\t  long long lastTestVerified = 0;\n1166\t  long long lastTestVerifiedFail = 0;\n1167\t  string lastDiagSig;\n1168\t  // the always-on net: the timestamp of the last verdict we have already acted\n1169\t  // on, and what that verdict was. Both are needed to spot the TRANSITION —\n1170\t  // \"it is red\" is noise if it was red an hour ago; \"it just turned red\" is the\n1171\t  // catch the whole product exists for.\n1172\t  long long lastNetTs = 0;\n1173\t  string lastNetVerdict;\n1174\t  Sess sess;                 // this process's own session, its own file\n1175\t  bool sessLoaded = false;\n1176\t\n1177\t  string sess_path() const { return sessDir + \"/\" + sessKey + \".json\"; }\n1178\t\n1179\t  // ---- R1.2: the move log ---------------------------------------------------\n1180\t  // One line per move, appended, never rewritten. Recording a move used to mean\n1181\t  // serialising the whole session object and replacing the file through a temp\n1182\t  // and a rename; with 200 moves that is 60 KB of write per tool event, and it\n1183\t  // measured 1.4 ms. An append is a write() of about 200 bytes.\n1184\t  //\n1185\t  // A COMPLETION IS AN APPEND TOO. PostToolUse learns the exit claim, the error\n1186\t  // signature and the suite verdict of a move that is already on disk. It does\n1187\t  // not seek back and patch that line — it appends a NEW line with the same\n1188\t  // seq, and the reader lets the later line win. Append-only means append-only;\n1189\t  // a log you edit in place is a file with extra steps and a torn-write window.\n1190\t  //\n1191\t  // NO FSYNC, ON PURPOSE, AND THE CHAIN IS WHY THAT IS ALLOWED. fsync on every\n1192\t  // tool event would hand back the millisecond this round exists to remove, and\n1193\t  // the thing being protected is a diagnostic record, not the user's source. The\n1194\t  // honest cost of that choice is that a crash can lose the tail of the log. So\n1195\t  // every line carries `prev`, the hash of the line before it: a lost or torn\n1196\t  // line is DETECTED rather than silently believed, which is the difference\n1197\t  // between a record with a known hole and a record that quietly lies. The\n1198\t  // policy and its numbers are written down in docs/butce.md.\n1199\t  string moves_path() const { return sessDir + \"/\" + sessKey + \".moves.bin\"; }\n1200\t  // R3: the tier-1 fingerprints, one sidecar slot per move seq. Beside the\n1201\t  // move ring rather than inside it — see the storage note in native/semantic.h.\n1202\t  string fps_path() const { return sessDir + \"/\" + sessKey + \".fp.bin\"; }\n1203\t\n1204\t  string lastMoveHash;\n1205\t\n1206\t  // ONE read of a KNOWN SIZE, then memcpy. No scanning, no field lookup, no\n1207\t  // per-field allocation. This is the whole reason the format changed: the work\n1208\t  // done here is the same on the first tool call of a session and on the four\n1209\t  // hundredth. reports/R1.3/accept.sh measures exactly that.\n1210\t  void load_moves() {\n1211\t    const int fd = open(moves_path().c_str(), O_RDONLY);\n1212\t    if (fd < 0) return;\n1213\t    rbmoves::Hdr h{};\n1214\t    if (pread(fd, &h, sizeof h, 0) != (ssize_t)sizeof h ||\n1215\t        memcmp(h.magic, \"RBMV1\", 5) != 0) { close(fd); return; }\n1216\t    const long long total = h.count;\n1217\t    const long long keep  = total < (long long)rbmoves::CAP ? total : (long long)rbmoves::CAP;\n1218\t    std::vector<rbmoves::Rec> recs((size_t)keep);\n1219\t    // The ring is contiguous on disk; read the whole thing in one call and pick\n1220\t    // the live window out of memory rather than issuing `keep` small reads.\n1221\t    if (keep > 0) {\n1222\t      std::vector<rbmoves::Rec> ring(rbmoves::CAP);\n1223\t      const ssize_t want = (ssize_t)(rbmoves::CAP * sizeof(rbmoves::Rec));\n1224\t      const ssize_t got = pread(fd, ring.data(), want, rbmoves::HDR_BYTES);\n1225\t      if (got <= 0) { close(fd); return; }\n1226\t      const long long first = total - keep;           // oldest surviving\n1227\t      for (long long i = 0; i < keep; i++)\n1228\t        recs[(size_t)i] = ring[(size_t)((first + i) % (long long)rbmoves::CAP)];\n1229\t    }\n1230\t    close(fd);\n1231\t\n1232\t    sess.moves.clear();\n1233\t    sess.moves.reserve(recs.size());\n1234\t    for (const auto& r : recs) {\n1235\t      rbmoves::Move m; rbmoves::from_rec(r, m);\n1236\t      sess.moves.push_back(m);\n1237\t    }\n1238\t    // raw text only on the newest RAW_KEEP — the reader defines the record, and\n1239\t    // every reader is handed the same thing (docs/butce.md).\n1240\t    if (sess.moves.size() > rbmoves::RAW_KEEP)\n1241\t      for (size_t k = 0; k + rbmoves::RAW_KEEP < sess.moves.size(); k++) sess.moves[k].raw.clear();\n1242\t\n1243\t    sess.nextSeq = h.nextSeq;\n1244\t    if (!sess.moves.empty()) {\n1245\t      rbmoves::Rec last; rbmoves::to_rec(sess.moves.back(), last);\n1246\t      lastMoveHash = rbsha::hex(string((const char*)&recs.back(), sizeof(rbmoves::Rec))).substr(0, 16);\n1247\t    }\n1248\t\n1249\t    // The chain is WRITTEN always and CHECKED only where checking is the point:\n1250\t    // under RABADON_MOVES_STRICT, and by rabadon-audit. A broken chain is a fact\n1251\t    // about the record, never a reason to refuse the user's command.\n1252\t    const char* sv = getenv(\"RABADON_MOVES_STRICT\");\n1253\t    if (sv && sv[0] == '1' && recs.size() > 1) {\n1254\t      for (size_t i = 1; i < recs.size(); i++) {\n1255\t        const string want = rbsha::hex(string((const char*)&recs[i - 1], sizeof(rbmoves::Rec))).substr(0, 16);\n1256\t        if (rbmoves::get(recs[i].prev, sizeof recs[i].prev) != want) {\n1257\t          fprintf(stderr, \"rabadon: move ring chain broken (a record is missing or was edited): %s\\n\",\n1258\t                  moves_path().c_str());\n1259\t          break;\n1260\t        }\n1261\t      }\n1262\t    }\n1263\t  }\n1264\t\n1265\t  // Append: write the record, THEN bump the count. A record half-written when\n1266\t  // the machine dies is a record outside the count, which means it never was.\n1267\t  void append_move(const rbmoves::Move& m) {\n1268\t    mkdir(sessDir.c_str(), 0755);\n1269\t    const int fd = open(moves_path().c_str(), O_RDWR | O_CREAT, 0644);\n1270\t    if (fd < 0) return;                    // fail open: never block on the record\n1271\t    rbmoves::Hdr h{};\n1272\t    if (pread(fd, &h, sizeof h, 0) != (ssize_t)sizeof h || memcmp(h.magic, \"RBMV1\", 5) != 0) {\n1273\t      memset(&h, 0, sizeof h);\n1274\t      memcpy(h.magic, \"RBMV1\", 5);\n1275\t      h.count = 0; h.nextSeq = 0;\n1276\t    }\n1277\t    rbmoves::Rec r; rbmoves::to_rec(m, r);\n1278\t    rbmoves::put(r.prev, sizeof r.prev, lastMoveHash);\n1279\t\n1280\t    // A completion rewrites the NEWEST record in place. Safe because it is the\n1281\t    // newest: nothing has chained to it yet, so no `prev` downstream goes stale.\n1282\t    const bool completing = (h.count > 0 && m.seq == h.nextSeq - 1);\n1283\t    const long long idx = completing ? (h.count - 1) % (long long)rbmoves::CAP\n1284\t                                     : h.count % (long long)rbmoves::CAP;\n1285\t    if (completing) {\n1286\t      rbmoves::Rec cur{};\n1287\t      if (pread(fd, &cur, sizeof cur, rbmoves::HDR_BYTES + idx * (long long)sizeof cur) == (ssize_t)sizeof cur)\n1288\t        memcpy(r.prev, cur.prev, sizeof r.prev);   // keep the link it was written with\n1289\t    }\n1290\t    if (pwrite(fd, &r, sizeof r, rbmoves::HDR_BYTES + idx * (long long)sizeof r) != (ssize_t)sizeof r) {\n1291\t      close(fd); return;\n1292\t    }\n1293\t    if (!completing) { h.count++; h.nextSeq = m.seq + 1; }\n1294\t    else if (h.nextSeq < m.seq + 1) h.nextSeq = m.seq + 1;\n1295\t    pwrite(fd, &h, sizeof h, 0);\n1296\t    close(fd);                             // no fsync: see docs/butce.md\n1297\t    lastMoveHash = rbsha::hex(string((const char*)&r, sizeof r)).substr(0, 16);\n1298\t  }\n1299\t\n1300\t  Sess& session() { return sess; }\n1301\t\n1302\t  // ---- the shared file -----------------------------------------------------\n1303\t  void load_shared(const string& j) {\n1304\t    if (j.empty()) return;\n1305\t    lastCodeEdit = get_num(j, \"lastCodeEdit\");\n1306\t    lastTestVerified = get_num(j, \"lastTestVerified\");\n1307\t    lastTestVerifiedFail = get_num(j, \"lastTestVerifiedFail\");\n1308\t    lastDiagAt   = get_num(j, \"lastDiagAt\");\n1309\t    lastDiagSig  = get_str(j, \"lastDiagSig\");\n1310\t    lastNetTs    = get_num(j, \"lastNetTs\");\n1311\t    lastNetVerdict = get_str(j, \"lastNetVerdict\");\n1312\t  }\n1313\t\n1314\t  static void read_sess(const string& obj, Sess& s) {\n1315\t    s.goalPrompt = get_str(obj, \"goalPrompt\");\n1316\t    s.goalTs = get_num(obj, \"goalTs\");\n1317\t    s.touchedDirs = parse_str_array(obj, \"touchedDirs\");\n1318\t    s.fanoutWarned = get_bool(obj, \"fanoutWarned\");\n1319\t    s.lastCmd = get_str(obj, \"lastCmd\");\n1320\t    s.lastCmdTs = get_num(obj, \"lastCmdTs\");\n1321\t    s.cmdRepeat = (int)get_num(obj, \"cmdRepeat\"); if (s.cmdRepeat < 1) s.cmdRepeat = 1;\n1322\t    s.actionCount = (int)get_num(obj, \"actionCount\");\n1323\t    s.offTarget = (int)get_num(obj, \"offTarget\");\n1324\t    s.walkCapWarned = (int)get_num(obj, \"walkCapWarned\");\n1325\t    s.driftChallenged = (int)get_num(obj, \"driftChallenged\");\n1326\t    for (const auto& r : parse_obj_array(obj, \"recent\"))\n1327\t      s.recent.push_back({ get_num(r, \"t\"), get_str(r, \"s\") });\n1328\t    s.recentEv = parse_str_array(obj, \"recentEv\");\n1329\t    s.tsOffset = get_num(obj, \"tsOffset\");\n1330\t    s.tokensOut = get_num(obj, \"tokensOut\");\n1331\t    s.tokensIn = get_num(obj, \"tokensIn\");\n1332\t    s.lastTestPass = get_num(obj, \"lastTestPass\");\n1333\t    s.lastTestFail = get_num(obj, \"lastTestFail\");\n1334\t    s.lastTestRun  = get_num(obj, \"lastTestRun\");\n1335\t    s.nextSeq = get_num(obj, \"nextSeq\");\n1336\t    // R4. injSeen is written as \"<name>=<count>\" strings so this hand-rolled\n1337\t    // serialiser needs no second array type; a session written before R4 has\n1338\t    // none of these keys and reads back as \"nothing injected yet\", which is the\n1339\t    // truth about it.\n1340\t    s.injPending = get_str(obj, \"injPending\");\n1341\t    s.injPendingSignal = get_str(obj, \"injPendingSignal\");\n1342\t    s.lastErrText = get_str(obj, \"lastErrText\");\n1343\t    // R5. A session written before R5 has none of these keys; get_num answers 0\n1344\t    // and get_str answers \"\", so injCapMoveSeq would read back as move 0 — \"the\n1345\t    // cap was reached at the very first move\", which is the ONE value that\n1346\t    // would make the trigger fire immediately on an upgraded session. The\n1347\t    // absent case is spelled out rather than defaulted.\n1348\t    s.repairFired = (int)get_num(obj, \"repairFired\");\n1349\t    s.injMuteSignal = get_str(obj, \"injMuteSignal\");\n1350\t    s.injMuteFromSeq = s.injMuteSignal.empty() ? -1 : (long long)get_num(obj, \"injMuteFromSeq\");\n1351\t    for (const auto& e : parse_str_array(obj, \"injSeen\")) {\n1352\t      const size_t eq = e.rfind('=');\n1353\t      if (eq == string::npos) continue;\n1354\t      s.injNames.push_back(e.substr(0, eq));\n1355\t      s.injCounts.push_back(atoi(e.c_str() + eq + 1));\n1356\t    }\n1357\t    // Sessions written before R1.2 carry their moves inline. Read them once so\n1358\t    // nobody's history resets at upgrade; they are never written back here, so\n1359\t    // the inline array drains into the log on the next append.\n1360\t    for (const auto& r : parse_obj_array(obj, \"moves\")) {\n1361\t      rbmoves::Move m;\n1362\t      m.seq = get_num(r, \"seq\");\n1363\t      m.ts  = get_num(r, \"ts\");\n1364\t      m.tool = get_str(r, \"tool\");\n1365\t      m.path = get_str(r, \"path\");\n1366\t      m.sig  = get_str(r, \"sig\");\n1367\t      m.raw  = get_str(r, \"raw\");\n1368\t      m.claimed_rc = (int)get_num(r, \"claimed_rc\");\n1369\t      m.err_sig = get_str(r, \"err_sig\");\n1370\t      m.suite = (int)get_num(r, \"suite\");\n1371\t      // A file written by an older build has no claimed_rc/suite keys, and\n1372\t      // get_num returns 0 for a key that is not there. 0 means \"green\" for\n1373\t      // suite and \"succeeded\" for claimed_rc, so reading a missing key as 0\n1374\t      // would invent results nobody measured. Absent stays unknown.\n1375\t      if (r.find(\"\\\"claimed_rc\\\"\") == string::npos) m.claimed_rc = -1;\n1376\t      if (r.find(\"\\\"suite\\\"\") == string::npos) m.suite = -1;\n1377\t      m.asserts = (int)get_num(r, \"asserts\");\n1378\t      if (r.find(\"\\\"asserts\\\"\") == string::npos) m.asserts = -1;\n1379\t      s.moves.push_back(m);\n1380\t    }\n1381\t    if (s.nextSeq == 0 && !s.moves.empty()) s.nextSeq = s.moves.back().seq + 1;\n1382\t  }\n1383\t\n1384\t  static string write_sess(const Sess& s) {\n1385\t    std::ostringstream o;\n1386\t    o << \"{\\\"goalPrompt\\\":\\\"\" << json_escape(s.goalPrompt) << \"\\\",\\\"goalTs\\\":\" << s.goalTs\n1387\t      << \",\\\"touchedDirs\\\":[\";\n1388\t    for (size_t i = 0; i < s.touchedDirs.size(); i++)\n1389\t      o << (i ? \",\" : \"\") << \"\\\"\" << json_escape(s.touchedDirs[i]) << \"\\\"\";\n1390\t    o << \"],\\\"fanoutWarned\\\":\" << (s.fanoutWarned ? \"true\" : \"false\")\n1391\t      << \",\\\"lastCmd\\\":\\\"\" << json_escape(s.lastCmd) << \"\\\",\\\"lastCmdTs\\\":\" << s.lastCmdTs\n1392\t      << \",\\\"cmdRepeat\\\":\" << s.cmdRepeat << \",\\\"actionCount\\\":\" << s.actionCount\n1393\t      << \",\\\"offTarget\\\":\" << s.offTarget << \",\\\"driftChallenged\\\":\" << s.driftChallenged\n1394\t      << \",\\\"walkCapWarned\\\":\" << s.walkCapWarned\n1395\t      << \",\\\"lastTestPass\\\":\" << s.lastTestPass << \",\\\"lastTestFail\\\":\" << s.lastTestFail\n1396\t      << \",\\\"lastTestRun\\\":\" << s.lastTestRun\n1397\t      << \",\\\"recent\\\":[\";\n1398\t    for (size_t i = 0; i < s.recent.size(); i++)\n1399\t      o << (i ? \",\" : \"\") << \"{\\\"t\\\":\" << s.recent[i].first << \",\\\"s\\\":\\\"\" << json_escape(s.recent[i].second) << \"\\\"}\";\n1400\t    o << \"],\\\"recentEv\\\":[\";\n1401\t    for (size_t i = 0; i < s.recentEv.size(); i++)\n1402\t      o << (i ? \",\" : \"\") << \"\\\"\" << json_escape(s.recentEv[i]) << \"\\\"\";\n1403\t    o << \"],\\\"injSeen\\\":[\";\n1404\t    for (size_t i = 0; i < s.injNames.size(); i++)\n1405\t      o << (i ? \",\" : \"\") << \"\\\"\" << json_escape(s.injNames[i]) << \"=\"\n1406\t        << (i < s.injCounts.size() ? s.injCounts[i] : 0) << \"\\\"\";\n1407\t    o << \"],\\\"injPending\\\":\\\"\" << json_escape(s.injPending) << \"\\\"\"\n1408\t      << \",\\\"injPendingSignal\\\":\\\"\" << json_escape(s.injPendingSignal) << \"\\\"\"\n1409\t      << \",\\\"lastErrText\\\":\\\"\" << json_escape(s.lastErrText) << \"\\\"\";\n1410\t    // R5, WRITTEN ONLY WHEN ARMED. Every other field in this object collapses\n1411\t    // to a zero/empty default when nothing has happened, and native/postuse_\n1412\t    // test.sh's differential relies on exactly that: a key the native gate\n1413\t    // writes as 0/\"\" reads as a key the retiring node gate never wrote, so\n1414\t    // parity of MEANING survives. injMuteFromSeq's unset value is -1, because\n1415\t    // move seq 0 is a real move and cannot double as \"never\" — and -1 is not a\n1416\t    // default, so writing it unconditionally made every session's state differ\n1417\t    // from node's for a fact node has no opinion about. It is written only once\n1418\t    // there is something to say. Absent reads back as -1 (see the loader).\n1419\t    if (s.injMuteFromSeq >= 0 || s.repairFired)\n1420\t      o << \",\\\"injMuteSignal\\\":\\\"\" << json_escape(s.injMuteSignal) << \"\\\"\"\n1421\t        << \",\\\"injMuteFromSeq\\\":\" << s.injMuteFromSeq\n1422\t        << \",\\\"repairFired\\\":\" << s.repairFired;\n1423\t    o << \",\\\"tsOffset\\\":\" << s.tsOffset << \",\\\"tokensOut\\\":\" << s.tokensOut\n1424\t      << \",\\\"tokensIn\\\":\" << s.tokensIn\n1425\t      << \",\\\"nextSeq\\\":\" << s.nextSeq << \"}\";\n1426\t    // R1.2: the moves themselves are NOT here any more. They live in an\n1427\t    // append-only log beside this file. Rewriting a 60 KB object to record one\n1428\t    // 200-byte fact cost 1.4 ms of every tool event; an append costs a write().\n1429\t    return o.str();\n1430\t  }\n1431\t\n1432\t  // Sessions that are still recorded inside state.json from before the split.\n1433\t  // Read for as long as they exist so nobody's counter resets at upgrade; never\n1434\t  // written back, so the map drains rather than lingering.\n1435\t  void migrate_from_shared(const string& j, const string& sid16) {\n1436\t    size_t sp = j.find(\"\\\"sessions\\\"\");\n1437\t    if (sp == string::npos) return;\n1438\t    const string smap = take_obj(j, j.find(':', sp + 10));\n1439\t    for (size_t i = 1; i + 1 < smap.size();) {\n1440\t      size_t q = smap.find('\"', i);\n1441\t      if (q == string::npos) break;\n1442\t      string key;\n1443\t      size_t q2 = q + 1;\n1444\t      for (; q2 < smap.size(); q2++) { if (smap[q2] == '\\\\') { q2++; continue; } if (smap[q2] == '\"') break; key += smap[q2]; }\n1445\t      size_t colon = smap.find(':', q2);\n1446\t      if (colon == string::npos) break;\n1447\t      size_t objAt = smap.find('{', colon);\n1448\t      const string obj = take_obj(smap, colon);\n1449\t      if (obj.empty() || objAt == string::npos) break;\n1450\t      if (key == sid16) { read_sess(obj, sess); return; }\n1451\t      i = objAt + obj.size();\n1452\t    }\n1453\t  }\n1454\t\n1455\t  // Anything untouched for a day goes. By TIME: \"keep the newest N\" is the rule\n1456\t  // that evicted the main session of a seven-way fan-out, and putting it back\n1457\t  // with a bigger N would only move the number the failure starts at. Swept at\n1458\t  // most once every ten minutes, tracked by the mtime of a marker, so 400 events\n1459\t  // in a row do not each walk the directory.\n1460\t  void sweep() const {\n1461\t    const string marker = sessDir + \"/.swept\";\n1462\t    struct stat st;\n1463\t    const long long now = now_ms();\n1464\t    if (stat(marker.c_str(), &st) == 0 &&\n1465\t        now - (long long)st.st_mtime * 1000 < SESSION_SWEEP_EVERY_MS) return;\n1466\t    { std::ofstream f(marker, std::ios::trunc); if (f) f << now; }\n1467\t    DIR* d = opendir(sessDir.c_str());\n1468\t    if (!d) return;\n1469\t    struct dirent* e;\n1470\t    while ((e = readdir(d))) {\n1471\t      const string n = e->d_name;\n1472\t      if (n.size() < 6 || n.compare(n.size() - 5, 5, \".json\") != 0) continue;\n1473\t      const string p = sessDir + \"/\" + n;\n1474\t      if (stat(p.c_str(), &st) != 0) continue;\n1475\t      if (now - (long long)st.st_mtime * 1000 > SESSION_TTL_MS) unlink(p.c_str());\n1476\t    }\n1477\t    closedir(d);\n1478\t  }\n1479\t\n1480\t  void load() {\n1481\t    const string j = read_file(path);\n1482\t    load_shared(j);\n1483\t    const string sj = read_file(sess_path());\n1484\t    if (!sj.empty()) { read_sess(sj, sess); sessLoaded = true; }\n1485\t    else migrate_from_shared(j, legacyKey);\n1486\t    load_moves();\n1487\t    sweep();\n1488\t  }\n1489\t\n1490\t  // R1.1 — DIRTY-TRACKED. The session file grew from a handful of counters to a\n1491\t  // 200-move record, and it was being serialised and written on every call to\n1492\t  // save(). Some events call save() more than once (the R1 recorder writes\n1493\t  // immediately, because every refusal path returns early and a record that\n1494\t  // survives only the allow path is a record of the wrong half; later branches\n1495\t  // then save again for their own reasons). That made one tool event cost two\n1496\t  // full rewrites of the largest file rabadon owns.\n1497\t  //\n1498\t  // The fix is not to record less. It is to write once: serialise, compare\n1499\t  // against what this process last wrote, and skip the syscall when the bytes\n1500\t  // did not move. The shared file below is small and still merged every time,\n1501\t  // because it has many writers and merging is what makes it correct\n1502\t  // without a lock.\n1503\t  //\n1504\t  // R1.3 — THE SHARED FILE IS DIRTY-TRACKED THE SAME WAY, AND THAT HALF WAS THE\n1505\t  // WHOLE BILL. R1.1 tracked the session file only, so the second save() an\n1506\t  // event makes still paid a temp-file-plus-rename on state.json. Measured\n1507\t  // in-process (instrumented copy, 120 events after a 200-event warm-up):\n1508\t  // save() cost 902 us across two calls with recording on against 458 us across\n1509\t  // one call with it off — +444 us for a write whose bytes were, on the second\n1510\t  // call, identical to the first.\n1511\t  //\n1512\t  // What is dirty-tracked is the MERGED result, not this object's fields. The\n1513\t  // merge is what makes this file correct without a lock — many sessions write\n1514\t  // it at once, so every save re-reads it and keeps the later of each stamp —\n1515\t  // and skipping on a pre-merge comparison would drop a concurrent writer's\n1516\t  // update on the floor. So the merge runs in full, every time, exactly as\n1517\t  // before; only the write is conditional, and its condition is \"the bytes I am\n1518\t  // about to write are already the bytes in the file\". When that holds, the\n1519\t  // write is a no-op by definition and the rename buys nothing. `cur` is read\n1520\t  // fresh on every call, so a concurrent writer's change makes the comparison\n1521\t  // fail and the write happen.\n1522\t  string lastSessBytes;\n1523\t  bool   lastSessValid = false;\n1524\t\n1525\t  // R1.3 — ONE EVENT, ONE SESSION-FILE WRITE.\n1526\t  //\n1527\t  // The recorder used to call save() the moment it had appended the move,\n1528\t  // because every refusal branch below it returns early and a record that only\n1529\t  // survives the allow path is a record of the wrong half of the session. A\n1530\t  // later branch then saved again, and that second save was a GENUINE write:\n1531\t  // actionCount, recent and lastCmd really do change in between, so the bytes\n1532\t  // differ and dirty-tracking cannot skip it. Two real writes for one event.\n1533\t  //\n1534\t  // The fix is not to record later, it is to WRITE later. The recorder now\n1535\t  // marks instead of writing, and the mark is discharged either by the next\n1536\t  // save() the event was going to make anyway — which then carries the record\n1537\t  // and the branch's own changes in the SAME write — or, on a path that makes\n1538\t  // no further save, by the guard in main() that runs on every exit.\n1539\t  //\n1540\t  // WHAT THE MARK DEFERS IS NOT THE RECORD. The move itself is already on disk\n1541\t  // before the mark is set: append_move() has written the ring record and its\n1542\t  // header. The only session-file field the recorder block touches is nextSeq,\n1543\t  // and load_moves() overwrites nextSeq from the ring header on every load, so\n1544\t  // the session file's copy is a duplicate of a fact that is already durable.\n1545\t  // That is why deferring is safe here and would not be for a field the ring\n1546\t  // does not carry.\n1547\t  bool pendingRecord = false;\n1548\t  void flush() { if (pendingRecord) save(); }\n1549\t\n1550\t  void save() {\n1551\t    pendingRecord = false;\n1552\t    const string body = write_sess(sess);"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CNJNRkZewzBF62my6wyhvM","session_id":"5cd1d3b8-0ab4-436c-a096-b92b12ed0757","uuid":"8c29e546-17e6-4239-be14-65c9a7fbd47e","timestamp":"2026-08-24T10:38:40.826Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Map gate.cpp hook path for daemon"}
