{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ru113sfa4HseMCJATnWjq6","type":"tool_result","content":"4312\t  auto ruleOff = [&](const char* id) {\n4313\t    if (sealed_rule(id)) return false;\n4314\t    for (const auto& d : disabled) if (d == id) return true;\n4315\t    return false;\n4316\t  };\n4317\t\n4318\t  // ---------- red base: the check is failing, so the run does not continue ----------\n4319\t  //\n4320\t  // The four complaints that produced this file were \"it catches nothing, it\n4321\t  // repairs nothing, on what grounds is unknown, when is unknown\". This is the\n4322\t  // first one. rabadon HAD the red — net.cpp ran the project's own check and\n4323\t  // wrote the verdict — and did nothing with it except print a sentence, once,\n4324\t  // on the transition, after the fact. The agent read the sentence and kept\n4325\t  // going. A supervisor whose strongest move is a suggestion is not supervision.\n4326\t  //\n4327\t  // The rule: while the project's own check is red, the next action does not\n4328\t  // start. Not \"is warned about\" — does not start. And not once, on the edge:\n4329\t  // EVERY action, for as long as the red stands, because a red that stops\n4330\t  // complaining after the first refusal is a red the agent can wait out.\n4331\t  //\n4332\t  // WHAT IS STILL ALLOWED, and why the carve-out is this shape. A stop with no\n4333\t  // way out is a wedge, and a wedged session ends with rabadon uninstalled, so\n4334\t  // the fix path stays wide open: reading anything, editing anything, and\n4335\t  // re-running the check. That is the complete set of moves needed to go from\n4336\t  // red to green, and it is also the exact set that CANNOT build on the broken\n4337\t  // base — a read commits nothing, an edit is the repair, and the check is what\n4338\t  // clears the refusal. Everything else — a commit, a push, an install, a\n4339\t  // deploy, spawning a sub-agent, moving on to the next feature — is work\n4340\t  // stacked on a base that is known to be broken, and every minute of it is\n4341\t  // paid for twice.\n4342\t  //\n4343\t  // The escape hatch is not a flag: it is running the check and seeing it pass.\n4344\t  // That matters for a flaky suite, which is the one input that could turn this\n4345\t  // law into the thing that kills the product. A flaky red clears itself the\n4346\t  // moment the check is re-run, and re-running the check is never refused.\n4347\t  if (netRed && hook == \"PreToolUse\" && !ruleOff(\"red-base\")) {\n4348\t    // the check command this project is judged by — the same string the\n4349\t    // contract printed at session start, so \"run the check\" is unambiguous.\n4350\t    const string checkCmd = get_str(read_file(cwd + \"/.rabadon/net.json\"), \"cmd\");\n4351\t    bool isFixPath = false;\n4352\t    // reads and edits: the two halves of repairing something.\n4353\t    if (toolName == \"Read\" || toolName == \"Grep\" || toolName == \"Glob\" ||\n4354\t        toolName == \"LS\" || toolName == \"NotebookRead\" || toolName == \"TodoWrite\" ||\n4355\t        toolName == \"Edit\" || toolName == \"Write\" || toolName == \"MultiEdit\" ||\n4356\t        toolName == \"NotebookEdit\")\n4357\t      isFixPath = true;\n4358\t    // and re-running the check itself, which is the ONLY way out of this\n4359\t    // refusal and therefore the one match that must not be pedantic. The first\n4360\t    // version compared the whole string: the check discovered as\n4361\t    // `npm test --silent` meant an agent that ran `npm test` — the same suite,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CNJNRkZewzBF62my6wyhvM","session_id":"5cd1d3b8-0ab4-436c-a096-b92b12ed0757","uuid":"94395979-555b-4324-9eb8-ec73b21d9fdd","timestamp":"2026-08-24T10:38:56.394Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Map gate.cpp hook path for daemon"}
